Written by Naomi Klein in 2007, The Shock Doctrine: The Rise of Disaster Capitalism argues that political leaders and corporations intentionally exploit collective crises—such as wars, coups, terrorist attacks, and natural disasters—to push through unpopular, radical free-market reforms.

Core Concept: Disaster Capitalism

• The Shock Metaphor: Klein compares economic shock therapy to electroshock psychiatry, where trauma temporarily disorients a subject, wiping away resistance so a new structure can be imposed.
• Exploiting Crises: When a society is paralyzed by disaster, citizens are too overwhelmed to protest, allowing elites to rapidly privatize public services, cut social spending, and deregulate markets.
• Milton Friedman's Influence: The book traces this ideology to economist Milton Friedman and the Chicago School, which championed aggressive, immediate market overhauls during moments of collapse.

Key Historical Examples

• Chile (1973): Following General Augusto Pinochet's bloody military coup, the "Chicago Boys" implemented sweeping free-market restructuring under a brutal dictatorship.

• The 1980s & 90s: Economic shock therapy spread to Latin America, post-Soviet Russia, and Poland, where hyperinflation and collapse were used to dismantle state-owned industries.

• Post-9/11 and Iraq (2003): The U.S. "War on Terror" and the invasion of Iraq accelerated the rise of private military contractors, security firms, and the mass privatization of state assets.

• Hurricane Katrina (2005): In New Orleans, flooded public schools and housing were rapidly converted into charter schools and private developments while residents remained displaced.

Reception and Impact

• Critique of Neoliberalism: The book established Klein as a leading voice against corporate globalization, detailing how disaster response itself has become a multi-billion-dollar    private industry.
• Public Debate: Supporters view it as a vital exposé on how political power manipulates trauma, while critics argue it oversimplifies complex historical transitions and economic pressure

